course: euphemisms
lesson: 34
title: Soft words for hard things: failure and mistakes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Saying "no" or "you got it wrong" can hurt, so people dress failure up in softer clothes: a letter that says "we've decided to go in a different direction" really means "you did not get the job".
explain: Some phrases are kind, like "not quite there yet" for a drawing that still needs work; others are funny, like "I dropped a clanger" for an embarrassing mistake.
explain: Businesses and politicians spin failures: a company whose plan flopped calls its new plan "a pivot", and a minister who was caught out says "lessons have been learned".
explain: Some phrases hide the truth completely: a person who "misremembered" may simply have said something false.
fact: "Dropped the ball" (mostly US) means made a mistake or failed to do what was needed.
fact: "Dropped a clanger" (UK slang) means made an embarrassing mistake.
fact: "A boo-boo" (humorous) means a small mistake.
fact: "An own goal" (UK) means a mistake that harms oneself.
fact: "Teething problems" (UK) means faults in something new.
fact: "A work in progress" means something not finished or not good yet.
fact: "Not quite there yet" means not good enough yet.
fact: "Back to the drawing board" means the plan has failed and we must start again.
fact: "That's one way of doing it" is a polite joke meaning you did it wrong.
fact: "An unforced error" (jargon, from tennis) means a mistake one made all by oneself.
fact: "A pivot" (US business jargon) means a change of plan after the first plan failed.
fact: "Lessons have been learned" (official) means we failed and we were blamed.
fact: "Unsuccessful", in a letter about a job or a place, means rejected.
fact: "We've decided to go in a different direction" means you have been rejected.
fact: "A misunderstanding" can hide a mistake or a quarrel.
fact: "Misremembered", said by an official, often means gave a false account.
fact: "A damp squib" (UK) means a disappointing failure, like a firework that does not go off.
fact: "Not ideal" (mostly UK) means bad.
fact: "Could have gone better" means went badly.
fact: "A mixed bag" means partly good and partly bad.
exercise: What does "I dropped the ball on your party invite" mean? | I made a mistake with your invite | I dropped a ball at your party | I threw the invite away on purpose | I played catch at your party
exercise: What does "he dropped a clanger at dinner" mean in Britain? | He made an embarrassing mistake | He dropped a heavy pan | He rang a loud bell | He broke a plate on purpose
exercise: A little brother says, "Oops, I made a boo-boo." What happened? | He made a small mistake | He hurt his knee badly | He saw a ghost | He played peekaboo
exercise: What does "that rule was an own goal for the council" mean? | The council's mistake hurt the council itself | The council scored in a football match | The council won a prize | The council did something very clever
exercise: What are "teething problems" with a new computer system? | Faults that come with something new | Problems with a baby's teeth | Problems with chewing | Bites from a pet
exercise: An artist says her painting is "a work in progress". What does she mean? | It is not finished yet | It is a famous work of art | It is about moving forward | It is already sold
exercise: The coach says your dive is "not quite there yet". What does he mean? | It is not good enough yet | It is perfect already | You dived into the wrong pool | You are not at the pool yet
exercise: What does "back to the drawing board" mean after a plan fails? | We must start again | We must draw a picture | We must buy a new board | We must go back to art class
exercise: Dad looks at your sandwich made with jam and ketchup: "Well, that's one way of doing it." What does he mean? | You did it wrong | You did it perfectly | That is the only way | He wants one too
exercise: What is "an unforced error" in tennis or in life? | A mistake you made all by yourself | A mistake someone forced you to make | A good shot that won the game | An error by the referee
exercise: A company that failed at selling games says it is making "a pivot" to selling toys. What is really happening? | It is changing plan after the first plan failed | It is spinning around in the office | It is selling more games than ever | It is building a new office
exercise: What does a minister mean by "lessons have been learned"? | We failed and we were blamed | We have been to school | We have taught a lesson | We have done nothing wrong
exercise: What does "your application was unsuccessful" mean? | You have been rejected | You have been accepted | Your letter was lost | You must apply twice
exercise: A job letter says, "We've decided to go in a different direction." What does it mean? | You did not get the job | The office is moving to a new town | They want you to drive them | You got the job
exercise: Two neighbours shouted at each other; later they call it "a misunderstanding". What really happened? | They had a quarrel | They did not hear each other | They spoke different languages | They were playing a game
exercise: A politician says he "misremembered" where he was. What may it really mean? | He said something false | He has a bad memory illness | He remembered too much | He told the truth twice
exercise: What does "the fireworks show was a damp squib" mean? | The show was a disappointing failure | The show was in the rain but great | The show had wet fireworks that were the best | The show was about squid
exercise: "Losing the car keys in the snow is not ideal." What does it really mean? | It is bad | It is perfect | It is a good idea | It is a little bit funny
exercise: The goalkeeper says the game "could have gone better". What does he mean? | It went badly | It went perfectly | It went on for too long | It was not played
exercise: What does "the reviews were a mixed bag" mean? | Some were good and some were bad | They were all wonderful | They were all put in one bag | They were all terrible
test: A school letter: "We regret to inform you that your application was unsuccessful." What does the family learn? | The child did not get a place | The child got a place | The letter was sent by mistake | They must write again next week
test: "Oi, you dropped a right clanger telling Gran about her surprise party!" What did the person do? | Made an embarrassing mistake | Dropped a heavy pot | Rang the doorbell | Planned the party well
test: News: "After the failure of its app, the company announced an exciting pivot." What really happened? | The company's first plan failed and it changed plan | The company built a spinning app | The company's app was a big hit | The company closed forever
test: "Our school play was a total damp squib, half the audience left." How did the play go? | It was a disappointing failure | It was a great success | It was cancelled by rain | It was about fireworks
test: Ben says: "'Teething problems' means a baby is getting teeth, so the new bus has babies on it?" Is he right? | No, it means faults in something new | Yes, babies are on the bus | Yes, the bus driver has toothache | No, it means the bus is old
test: The minister on TV: "Mistakes were made, and lessons have been learned." What is she admitting? | That they failed | That they did nothing wrong | That they will go back to school | That they won an award
test: "he says he misremembered the size of the fish lol, it was a goldfish" What really happened? | He told a false story | He forgot what fish are | He caught a huge fish | He remembered it exactly
test: "Footie match? Could've gone better. We lost 5–0." How did the match go? | Badly | Very well | It was a draw | It was cancelled
test: The film director writes back: "Thanks, but we've decided to go in a different direction." What does the actor learn? | He did not get the part | He got the part | The film is set on a road trip | He must travel abroad
test: "Mum, I made a boo-boo — the cat's food went in the fish tank." How big is the mistake meant to sound? | Small and silly | Huge and dangerous | Not a mistake at all | Clever and planned
test: The robot fell over during the contest. "Back to the drawing board, team." What does the leader mean? | We must start again | We must draw the robot | The robot is perfect | We have won
test: "Missed the open goal, that's an unforced error, you muppet!" Whose fault was the miss? | The player's own fault | The other team's fault | The referee's fault | The wind's fault
exam: Whereas correspondence informs a candidate that the organisation "has decided to go in a different direction", identify the outcome conveyed thereby. | The candidate's application has been rejected | The organisation has relocated its premises | The candidate has been appointed to the position | The organisation requests the candidate's travel itinerary
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression "a damp squib" as employed in respect of a public event in the United Kingdom. | A disappointing failure | An event curtailed by inclement weather that nonetheless succeeded | An exhibition of marine fauna | An event of exceptional brilliance
exam: It is given that a public official asserts that he "misremembered" the circumstances of an occurrence. Which of the following most accurately characterises the likely import of this assertion? | The official provided a false account | The official suffers from a diagnosed disorder of memory | The official provided an exceptionally accurate account | The official declined to provide any account
exam: Determine the meaning of the term "teething problems" when applied to a newly installed system. | Faults arising in something recently introduced | Dental complaints among the system's operators | Defects arising from excessive age | Difficulties associated with the mastication of food
